            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "The document is a case report detailing two adverse reactions related to the medication Neptazol, which is prescribed for hypertension. \n\n**Case 1** describes a 52-year-old woman who developed a generalized urticarial rash and mild lip swelling after ten days of taking Neptazol 20mg daily. She was treated with antihistamines, and her symptoms resolved within 48 hours after discontinuation of the medication.\n\n**Case 2** involves a 67-year-old man who experienced facial angioedema and difficulty swallowing after three weeks on the same dosage of Neptazol. He required emergency treatment with intramuscular epinephrine and intravenous corticosteroids, and he was hospitalized overnight. Neptazol was permanently discontinued for this patient.\n\nBoth cases demonstrate serious allergic reactions to Neptazol, suggesting the need for caution and monitoring when using this medication. \n\nThis document is relevant to patient safety as it provides insights into adverse drug reactions, highlighting the importance of pharmacovigilance in identifying and managing risks associated with medications. It may inform future decisions regarding the safety of Neptazol and the need for clearer patient warnings or monitoring protocols.",
